{"id": "43", "pred": "Alternatively, if the plastic is difficult to remove:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If complete removal is not possible, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and consult a service technician for the repair.", "raw_output": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the softened plastic off using a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, being careful not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nAlternatively, if the plastic is difficult to remove:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If complete removal is not possible, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer and consult a service technician for the repair.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to get plastic out of a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n4. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic is still difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun before attempting to scrape it off. However, if t