{"id": "82", "pred": "Causes of IBS are not fully understood but are believed to result from several factors, including abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements (such as too fast, slow, or overly strong colon and small intestine contractions), increased awareness or hypersensitivity to bodily functions, altered communication between the brain and the GI tract, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "raw_output": "Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often violent episodes), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps—typically in the lower abdomen—that worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement, as well as excessive gas or bloating.\n\nCauses of IBS are not fully understood but are believed to result from several factors, including abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements (such as too fast, slow, or overly strong colon and small intestine contractions), increased awareness or hypersensitivity to bodily functions, altered communication between the brain and the GI tract, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities possibly due to poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_catego