{"id": "104", "pred": "The body systems work together by supporting and maintaining each other's functions—such as the urinary system cleaning blood and removing waste from other systems, and the respiratory system providing oxygen and removing carbon dioxide from various systems.", "raw_output": "The body systems work together to maintain normal functioning. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from multiple systems including the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. Similarly, the respiratory system supplies oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from systems such as the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems. These interactions show how closely interconnected the body's systems are in supporting overall health and function. Answer: The body systems work together by supporting and maintaining each other's functions—such as the urinary system cleaning blood and removing waste from other systems, and the respiratory system providing oxygen and removing carbon dioxide from various systems.", "gt": "The body systems work together in order to maintain the normal functioning of the human body. These systems include the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, urinary, endocrine, lymphatic, immune, reproductive, and nervous systems. Each system has its own specific functions, but they also work in conjunction with one another to ensure the overall well-being of the body. For example, the urinary system cleans waste products from various systems such as the skeletal, muscular, digestive, respiratory, circulatory, and nervous systems. Similarly, the respiratory system provides oxygen to all body parts and removes carbon dioxide from the muscular, digestive, skeletal, circulatory, urinary, and nervous systems.", "question": "how the body systems work together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "ha