{"id": "140", "pred": "According to the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. Under the block concept, all assets within the same class and subject to the same depreciation rate are clubbed together as a single asset. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening Written Down Value (WDV) of the asset less its scrap value, even if the asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.", "raw_output": "According to the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. Under the block concept, all assets within the same class and subject to the same depreciation rate are clubbed together as a single asset. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening Written Down Value (WDV) of the asset less its scrap value, even if the asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be deducted from the opening work-in-progress (WDV) of such asset in the previous year in which it was discarded. This reduction is taken into account while computing the depreciation expense for the subsequent years. However, any changes made to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of Income Tax Act 1961.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": 