{"id": "166", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, noncancerous conditions such as prostatitis (inflammation of the prostate), or benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), which is an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the ACS, along with BPH or inflammation. Additionally, if digital rectal exam (DRE) results are normal but PSA is moderately elevated, a free PSA test may be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "raw_output": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, noncancerous conditions such as prostatitis (inflammation of the prostate), or benign prostate hyperplasia (BPH), which is an enlarged prostate. For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer according to the ACS, along with BPH or inflammation. Additionally, if digital rectal exam (DRE) results are normal but PSA is moderately elevated, a free PSA test may be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, PSA levels refer to the amount of prostate-specific antigen present in a man's blood. The level of PSA is measured in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Normal PSA levels range between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml, and a moderate elevation in PSA levels may require further testing, such as a free PSA test, to determine the ratio of free to total PSA and help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA. Therefore, the PSA test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and to detect elevated PSA levels that may indicate prostate cancer or other prostate issues.", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 60.99, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [5012, 32, 5866, 8300, 311, 279, 19950, 315, 46347, 18906, 81989, 11, 264, 12833, 8947, 553, 279, 46347, 66069, 11, 16878, 304, 264, 883, 748, 6543, 13, 576, 3059, 525, 11136, 4961, 304, 20021, 55385, 315, 69486, 817, 2544, 321, 2015, 320, 968, 92322, 8, 315, 6543, 13, 96593, 69486, 5866, 1231, 13216, 46347, 9387, 11, 2477, 66, 11705, 782, 4682, 1741, 438, 462, 9878, 19435, 320, 258, 44764, 367, 315, 279, 46347, 701, 476, 64209, 46347, 17071, 500, 35159, 320, 33, 10842, 701, 892, 374, 458, 73017, 46347, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 2790, 69486, 5866, 1948, 220, 19, 13, 15, 7777, 92322, 323, 220, 16, 15, 13, 15, 7777, 92322, 1231, 4190, 264, 220, 17, 20, 4, 6012, 315, 46347, 9387, 4092, 311, 279, 78084, 11, 3156, 448, 425, 10842, 476, 36040, 13, 22406, 11, 421, 7377, 7608, 278, 7006, 320, 35, 787, 8, 3059, 525, 4622, 714, 69486, 374, 69251, 31289, 11, 264, 1910, 69486, 1273, 1231, 387, 1483, 311, 8552, 279, 11341, 315, 1910, 311, 2790, 69486, 311, 1492, 53163, 1948, 9387, 323, 1008, 2477, 1786, 11705, 11137, 315, 31289, 69486, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.2526, -0.0, -0.0623, -0.0009, 0.0, -0.0, -0.165, -0.0, -0.0791, -0.0, -0.0, -0.2154, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0015, -0.0, -0.0072, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1006, -0.0, -0.7178, -0.0, -0.1602, -0.0002, -0.1493, -0.4495, -0.