{"id": "207", "pred": "Swiss chard, also known as plain chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach in appearance but has a stronger, more assertive, and sometimes bitter flavor. Although unrelated to spinach, chard belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars, being a cultivated descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima. Chard is selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation. It can have fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks and adds color to the vegetable garden. Young chard can be eaten raw in salads, while mature leaves and stalks are typically cooked, which reduces bitterness and refines the flavor. Chard is perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for up to 2 to 3 days.", "raw_output": "Swiss chard, also known as plain chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It is similar to spinach in appearance but has a stronger, more assertive, and sometimes bitter flavor. Although unrelated to spinach, chard belongs to the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars, being a cultivated descendant of the sea beet, Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima. Chard is selected for leaf production rather than storage root formation. It can have fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks and adds color to the vegetable garden. Young chard can be eaten raw in salads, while mature leaves and stalks are typically cooked, which reduces bitterness and refines the flavor. Chard is perishable and should be stored unwashed in plastic bags in the crisper for up to 2 to 3 days.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 63.015, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [13218, 1038, 521, 567, 11, 1083, 3881, 438, 14396, 521, 567, 11, 374, 264, 35481, 448, 3460, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 323, 12045, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 13, 1084, 374, 4428, 311, 67698, 304, 11094, 714, 702, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 11, 323, 7025, 25180, 17172, 13, 10328, 45205, 311, 67698, 11, 521, 567, 17180, 311, 279, 1852, 5147, 88621, 438, 48012, 2888, 320, 70, 8341, 48012, 8, 323, 678, 1008, 48012, 26883, 1561, 11, 1660, 264, 66066, 75851, 315, 279, 9396, 48012, 11, 34243, 73160, 285, 1186, 2154, 13, 3594, 275, 7523, 13, 910, 567, 374, 4091, 369, 15933, 5670, 4751, 1091, 5819, 3704, 18003, 13, 1084, 646, 614, 7722, 4158, 11, 13753, 11, 476, 2518, 15933, 54872, 82, 323, 11367, 1894, 311, 279, 35481, 13551, 13, 13263, 521, 567, 646, 387, 34561, 7112, 304, 76752, 11, 1393, 14851, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 11, 892, 25271, 78996, 323, 2053, 1543, 279, 17172, 13, 910, 567, 374, 82117, 480, 323, 1265, 387, 9768, 15025, 13571, 304