{"id": "224", "pred": "If the toilet flushes slowly, tighten the chain slightly on the lift arm.", "raw_output": "To fix the lift valve (flapper valve and chain) in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Connect the new chain to the third hole on the lift arm—the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm—using pliers to secure it firmly.\n5. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included, tighten it securely until there is no more give.\n6. Turn the water valve back on to fill the tank.\n7. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\n8. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\n9. Once satisfied with the operation, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent tangling.\n\nIf the toilet flushes slowly, tighten the chain slightly on the lift arm.", "gt": "To fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Turn off the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms.\n3. Remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n4. Fill the tank by turning on the water valve and then flushing the toilet. Adjust the lift chain as needed by moving the metal clip up or down a few links. Cut off any excess lift chain to prevent tangling.\n5. Push the solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle and connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. Make sure it is securely fastened.\n6. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If it flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it tighter on the lift arm.", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 67.85,