{"id": "240", "pred": "Crab traps work by allowing crabs to enter but preventing them from exiting. They typically feature leads (angled entrances) from the sides and bottom that guide crabs toward a narrow slit, which is the only entrance into the trap. Once inside, crabs are unable to escape due to the design of the trap. These traps are constructed with a metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars, and often include nets inside. The design exploits crabs' behavior, such as their tendency to follow food, luring them into the pot where they become trapped.", "raw_output": "Crab traps work by allowing crabs to enter but preventing them from exiting. They typically feature leads (angled entrances) from the sides and bottom that guide crabs toward a narrow slit, which is the only entrance into the trap. Once inside, crabs are unable to escape due to the design of the trap. These traps are constructed with a metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars, and often include nets inside. The design exploits crabs' behavior, such as their tendency to follow food, luring them into the pot where they become trapped.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a crab trap is a large square metal cage with a system of nets inside that allows crabs to enter through a narrow slit at the top. To set the trap, the ends with lead-ins are placed in the water and the crabs are attracted to the free meal offered by the trap. The Alaska Department of Fish and Game recommends using king crab pots that range from 6 by 6 feet to 8 by 8 feet with heights between 2 and 3 feet. Over time, these mechanisms may disable the trap, preventing it from ghost fishing.", "question": "how do crab traps work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 39.765, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [16001, 370, 44560, 975, 553, 10693, 1