{"id": "242", "pred": "Crab traps work by allowing crabs to enter but preventing them from exiting. They typically feature leads (angled entrances) from the sides and bottom that guide crabs toward a narrow slit, which is the only entrance into the trap. Once inside, crabs are unable to escape due to the design of the trap. These traps are constructed with a metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars, and often include nets inside. The design exploits crabs' behavior, such as their tendency to follow food, luring them into the pot where they become trapped.", "raw_output": "Crab traps work by allowing crabs to enter but preventing them from exiting. They typically feature leads (angled entrances) from the sides and bottom that guide crabs toward a narrow slit, which is the only entrance into the trap. Once inside, crabs are unable to escape due to the design of the trap. These traps are constructed with a metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh reinforced with steel bars, and often include nets inside. The design exploits crabs' behavior, such as their tendency to follow food, luring them into the pot where they become trapped.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how crab traps work:\n\nCrab traps are made of a metal cage with a system of nets inside, which are designed to allow crabs to enter but not escape. The traps have two leads that angle into the pot from the sides and bottom, leading to a narrow slit entrance. The crabs crawl up the leads to reach the bait inside the pot, but cannot exit through the slit. The pot is designed to keep the crabs inside until they can be retrieved by the fisherman.\n\nAdditionally, according to passage 3, crab pots can be equipped with mechanisms that disable them over time, preventing them from continuing to catch crabs after they have been abandoned or become lost. These mechanisms can include biodegradable mesh or dissolvable cords.", "question": "how do crab traps work", "group_key"