{"id": "244", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another key difference is in the definition of disability: under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas under FEHA, only a limitation (not necessarily substantial) on a major life activity is required.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another key difference is in the definition of disability: under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas under FEHA, only a limitation (not necessarily substantial) on a major life activity is required.", "gt": "The FEHA (Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) have several differences. The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another important difference is the requirement of disability. The ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity, while the FEHA only requires that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, without the need for it to be a substantial limit.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 31.393, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [3966, 6672, 1948, 27931, 17020, 323, 78325, 374, 429, 279, 78325, 16