{"id": "246", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another key difference is in the definition of disability: under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas under FEHA, only a limitation (not necessarily substantial) on a major life activity is required.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another key difference is in the definition of disability: under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment must substantially limit a major life activity to qualify as a disability, whereas under FEHA, only a limitation (not necessarily substantial) on a major life activity is required.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) are:\n\n1. Scope of application: The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\n2. Definition of disability: The FEHA requires that a disability must limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires that a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\n3. Number of employees: The ADA applies to employers with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA has no such requirement.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between FEHA and ADA is the scope of application and the definition of disability.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "datase