{"id": "409", "pred": "Push chunks or torn pieces of white bread through a food processor's shredding disk to", "raw_output": "Based on the provided passages, to make coarse bread crumbs similar to panko, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Push torn or chunked pieces of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs.\n3. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet (or two half sheet pans in passage 3).\n4. Bake for 6 to 8 minutes (or about 5 minutes in passage 3) until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, or until they just begin to brown and become crisp.\n5. Shake the sheet(s) twice during baking to ensure even drying or browning.\n6. Remove from the oven and cool completely.\n7. Store in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nNote: The method described in the passages makes coarse, oven-dried bread crumbs, which are similar in texture to panko but may not be identical to commercially produced panko, which is typically made from crustless bread and undergoes a specific pressing process. However, these homemade crumbs serve a similar culinary purpose.\n\nAnswer: Push chunks or torn pieces of white bread through a food processor's shredding disk to", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\nPassage 1 states to push torn pieces of bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 2 provides similar instructions, stating to push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300°F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional information, suggesting to preheat the oven to 300°F before making the crumbs. The passage also recommends using a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form, and then spreading the crumbs evenly on two half-sheet pans and baking until they just begin to brown and are crisp, about 5 minutes. After cooling the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, transfer them to an airtight container and store them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nTherefore, to make panko crumbs based on the provided passages, you can use a food processor or blender to shred or pulse bread into coarse crumbs, and then bake them in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 83.572, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 3897, 46769, 11, 311, 1281, 49247, 16002, 96577, 4428, 311, 281, 92852, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 4968, 19963, 279, 23387, 311, 220, 18, 15, 15, 12348, 434, 320, 16, 20, 15, 12348, 356, 4292, 17, 13, 22950, 21145, 476, 11879, 291, 9666, 315, 4158, 16002, 1526, 279, 52362, 287, 13364, 315, 264, 3607, 17654, 311, 1281, 49247, 96577, 624, 18, 13, 47716, 279, 96577, 41047, 389, 264, 27815, 10834, 320, 269, 1378, 4279, 10834, 63783, 304, 21085, 220, 18, 4292, 19, 13, 52074, 369, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 4420, 320, 269, 911, 220, 20, 4420, 304, 21085, 220, 18, 8, 3080, 279, 96577, 525, 9058, 714, 537, 92872, 11, 476, 3080, 807, 1101, 3161, 311, 13876, 323, 3635, 41854, 624, 20, 13, 72323, 279, 10834, 1141, 8, 10917, 2337, 27815, 311, 5978, 1496, 45379, 476, 293, 50420, 624, 21, 13, 10783, 504, 279, 23387, 323, 7010, 6587, 624, 22, 13, 9129, 304, 458, 264, 2106, 491, 5476, 518, 3054, 9315, 369, 705, 311, 220, 17, 5555, 382, 9112, 25, 576, 1714, 7481, 304, 279, 46769, 3643, 49247, 11, 23387, 1737, 4487, 16002, 96577, 11, 892, 525, 4428, 304, 10434, 311, 281, 92852, 714, 1231, 537, 387, 19516, 311, 53353, 8947, 281, 9285