{"id": "432", "pred": "Use warm soapy water and a soft cloth for mild tarnish; for heavier tarnish, apply a baking soda paste with a lint-free cloth; alternatively, try a saltwater bath for lightly", "raw_output": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, the method depends on the severity of the tarnish and the type of jewelry:\n\nFor lightly tarnished silver: Use a salt bath by pouring two cups of hot water into a bowl (enough to cover the jewelry). This gentle cleanser removes tarnish without abrasion. For just one piece, use less water; for multiple pieces, use more. However, if the jewelry has gemstones, ensure they won't be affected by submersion in a saltwater solution.\n\nFor heavier tarnish on silver: Create a paste using three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (avoid paper towels, as they can scratch).\n\nFor dull or filmy silver: Mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth, rub the jewelry, rinse in cool water, and buff dry with a cloth.\n\nFor diamonds: Clean with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat dry with a soft cloth.\n\nAnswer: Use warm soapy water and a soft cloth for mild tarnish; for heavier tarnish, apply a baking soda paste with a lint-free cloth; alternatively, try a saltwater bath for lightly", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a few different methods based on the passages provided. For silver jewelry, you can mix mild dish soap with warm water, dip a soft cloth into the mixture, and rub the jewelry. Then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water, apply it to the silver with a soft cloth, and rinse it off. For diamonds, you can clean them with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, then pat them dry with a soft cloth. If you have silver jewelry with gemstones, make sure they 