{"id": "450", "pred": "A caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting from volcanic activity. In contrast, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed due to sinking in of rocks because of high pressure. Additionally, passage 2 notes that craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, which is not a formation method for calderas.", "raw_output": "A caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting from volcanic activity. In contrast, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed due to sinking in of rocks because of high pressure. Additionally, passage 2 notes that craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, which is not a formation method for calderas.", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression caused by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava gets emptied. This cavity creates pressure and the overground rocks collapse to create a large depression. On the other hand, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the opening that is used for the eruption of magma and lava. This is a depression that is the result of sinking in of the rocks because of high pressure and it typically has the opening through which lava and ash flows upwards. Craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, and can range in size from very small to several hundred km across.", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 38.189, "status": "success", "record_