{"id": "451", "pred": "A caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting from volcanic activity. In contrast, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed due to sinking in of rocks because of high pressure. Additionally, passage 2 notes that craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, which is not a formation method for calderas.", "raw_output": "A caldera is different from a crater in how they are formed. A caldera is a large (at least one km across), circular depression formed at the top of a volcano when the magma chamber collapses, resulting from volcanic activity. In contrast, a volcanic crater is a bowl-like structure at the top of a volcano around the eruption opening, formed due to sinking in of rocks because of high pressure. Additionally, passage 2 notes that craters can also be caused by impacts from meteorites, which is not a formation method for calderas.", "gt": "A caldera is a large depression created by volcanic activity when a chamber of magma and lava empties, causing the overlying rocks to collapse. It is formed by the collapsing of the ground. On the other hand, a crater is a bowl-shaped structure at the top of a volcano around the opening used for the eruption of magma and lava. It is formed by the sinking in of rocks due to high pressure.", "question": "how is a caldera different from a crater", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 38.046, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 1620, 97903, 374, 2155, 504, 264, 74707, 304, 1246, 807, 525, 14122, 13, 362, 1620, 97903, 374, 264, 3460, 320, 266, 3245, 825, 13136, 3941, 701, 26931, 18210, 14122, 518, 279, 1909, 315, 264, 65206, 979, 279, 63505, 24