{"id": "516", "pred": "The word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins. The first known reference to the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC, where he observed an occulting star in Gemini and spoke of Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "raw_output": "The word \"Gemini\" is a Latin word for twins. The first known reference to the Gemini constellation was in Aristotle’s Meteorologica, over 300 years BC, where he observed an occulting star in Gemini and spoke of Jupiter in conjunction with it.", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "question": "history of the word gemini", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 20.319, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [785, 3409, 330, 95040, 6591, 1, 374, 264, 19458, 3409, 369, 43636, 13, 576, 1156, 3881, 5785, 311, 279, 85711, 82386, 572, 304, 77901, 748, 39632, 1609, 3001, 11, 916, 220, 18, 15, 15, 1635, 18040, 11, 1380, 566, 13166, 458, 73826, 287, 6774, 304, 85711, 323, 12290, 315, 49689, 304, 31446, 448, 432, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.0298, -0.0006, -0.5763, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0161, -0.4848, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.3144, -0.1002, -0.0072, -0.4101, -0.0107, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0046, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3909, -0.0398, 0.0, -0.0019, -0.0087, -0.0