{"id": "551", "pred": "The collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. β is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in high-power transistors. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT (bipolar junction", "raw_output": "A transistor is a miniature electronic component and a semiconductor device used to amplify and switch electronic signals and electrical power. It is composed of semiconductor material with at least three terminals for connection to an external circuit—typically the emitter, base, and collector.\n\nA transistor can function either as an amplifier or a switch. When it works as an amplifier, it takes in a small electric current (input current) at one end (the base) and produces a much larger electric current (output current) at the other end (the collector). This amplification occurs because the base pulls electrons from the emitter due to having a more positive voltage, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor from the emitter to the collector.\n\nIn its switching function, normally holes in the base act as a barrier, preventing significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is off. When activated, electrons and holes move across the two junctions between n-type and p-type silicon, allowing current to flow.\n\nThe collector current is approximately β (common-emitter current gain) times the base current. β is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in high-power transistors. Unlike field-effect transistors, the BJT (bipolar junction", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how a transistor works:\n\n1. A transistor functions as both an amplifier and a switch.\n2. The transistor's design allows it to control a larger supply of electricity with a small amount of electricity, much like turning a valve to control a supply of water.\n3. The base of the transistor pulls electrons from the emitter, creating a flow of electricity through the transistor.\n4. The transistor can take in a tiny electric current at one end (the input current) and produce a much bigger electric current at the other end (the output current) when it works as an amplifier.\n5. The holes in the base of the transistor act as a barrier, preventing any significant current flow from the emitter to the collector when the transistor is in its off state.\n6. When the electrons and holes move across the two junctions between the n-type and p-type silicon, the transistor begins to work.\n7. The collector current is approximately beta (common-emitter current gain) times the base current, and it is typically greater than 100 for small-signal transistors but can be smaller in transistors designed for high-power applications.\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages.", "question": "what is a transistor how does it work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 82.509, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [32, 97941, 374, 264, 56763, 14346, 3692, 323, 264, 86736, 3671, 1483, 311, 96068, 323, 3398, 14346, 17289, 323, 19734, 2355, 13, 1084, 374, 23415, 315, 86736, 3684, 448, 518, 3245, 2326, 52979, 369, 3633, 311, 458, 9250, 16224, 2293, 86084, 279, 47263, 11, 2331, 11, 323, 31953, 382, 32, 97941, 646, 729, 2987, 438, 458, 60066, 476, 264, 3398, 13, 3197, 432, 4278, 438, 458, 60066, 11, 432, 4990, 304, 264, 2613, 9072, 1482, 320, 1355, 1482, 8, 518, 825, 835, 320, 1782, 2331, 8, 323, 18644, 264, 1753, 8131, 9072, 1482, 320, 3006, 1482, 8, 518, 279, 1008, 835, 320, 1782, 31953, 568, 1096, 22395, 2404, 13666, 1576, 279, 2331, 33045, 56578, 504, 279, 47263, 4152, 311, 3432, 264, 803, 6785, 21720, 11, 6825, 264, 6396, 315, 17728, 1526, 279, 97941, 504, 279, 47263, 311, 279, 31953, 382, 641, 1181, 27765, 729, 11, 14279, 19766, 304, 279, 2331, 1160, 438, 264, 22103, 11,