{"id": "664", "pred": "When you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "raw_output": "When you breathe in (inhale), the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts by pulling downward, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "gt": "When you breathe in, or inhale, the intercostal muscles contract and expand the ribcage while the diaphragm contracts and pulls downward to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest and allows air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe. When you breathe out, or exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles also relax. This reduces the space in the chest cavity and forces out air that is rich in carbon dioxide out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 50.671, "status": "success", "re