{"id": "665", "pred": "When you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "raw_output": "When you breathe in (inhale), the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts by pulling downward, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "gt": "When you breathe in, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm also contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. This forces air rich in carbon dioxide out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 50.618, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 320, 20868, 1574, 701, 2