{"id": "666", "pred": "When you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "raw_output": "When you breathe in (inhale), the intercostal muscles contract to expand the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts by pulling downward, increasing the volume of the chest cavity. This lowers the pressure inside the chest, allowing air to be sucked into the lungs through the windpipe.\n\nWhen you breathe out (exhale), the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of the lungs, through the windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth. Breathing out generally requires no effort unless there is a lung disease or physical activity.", "gt": "When you breathe in, your diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, expanding the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract to expand the ribcage. Pressure inside the chest is reduced, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. On the other hand, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. Air rich in carbon dioxide is then forced out of the lungs and windpipe. Breathing out does not require any effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 50.988, "status": "s