{"id": "701", "pred": "Additionally, teachers can enhance learning by providing engaging materials and asking open-ended questions, modeling vocabulary, encouraging predictions, and observing children's activities.", "raw_output": "Children learn several things through water play:\n\n1. Gross motor skills: Running through sprinklers or filling and pouring water helps develop physical coordination and movement.\n2. Academic learning:\n   - Science: Children learn about floating and sinking and how different objects behave in water.\n   - Math: They learn about measuring, more and less by filling buckets with varying amounts of water.\n3. Creativity: Water play encourages imagination and pretend play.\n\nAdditionally, teachers can enhance learning by providing engaging materials and asking open-ended questions, modeling vocabulary, encouraging predictions, and observing children's activities.", "gt": "Children learn various skills and concepts through water play. It aids in the development of gross motor skills as they engage in activities like running through sprinklers or filling and pouring water from buckets. Water play also facilitates academic learning in areas such as science and math. Children learn about the concept of floating and sinking, and understand that different objects have different buoyancies. In math, they learn about measurement by determining the amount of water that fits into a bucket, and concepts of more and less by comparing water levels in different buckets. Water play also promotes creativity, allowing children to use their imagination.", "question": "what do children learn with water play", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 39.25, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [11539, 3960, 3807, 2513, 1526, 3015, 1486, 1447, 16, 13, 42807, 8872, 7361, 25, 28025, 1526, 50698, 9254, 476, 21274, 323, 50413, 3015, 8609, 2225, 6961, 37693, 323, 7203, 624, 17, 13, 41070, 6832, 510, 256, 481, 9965, 25, 15044, 3960, 911, 19057, 323, 61093, 323, 1246, 2155, 6171, 35692, 304, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 4149, 25, 2379, 3960, 911, 28990, 11, 803, 323, 2686, 553, 21274, 42112, 448, 28765, 14713, 315, 3015, 624, 18, 13, 17667, 1927, 25, 9959, 1486, 36067, 27799, 323, 34740, 1486, 382, 49574, 11, 13336, 646, 18379, 6832, 553, 8241, 22570, 7236, 323, 10161, 1787, 83075, 4755, 11, 33479, 34918, 11, 25836, 19898, 11, 323, 44971, 2841, 594, 7488, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.07, -0.0, -0.5313, -0.0117, -0.5831, -0.0, -0.0, -0.3227, -0.0894, -0.0, -0.0071, -0.0142, -0.0001, -0.5622, -0.3762, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.1474, -0.0033, -0.0011, -0.001, -0.1031, -0.1772, -0.2289, -0.3363, -0.1402, -0.6395, -0.5968, -0.5569, -0.0123, -0.0, -0.0403, -0.0925, -0.5645, -0.0011, -0.0026, -0.4747, -0.0008, -0.6634, -0.156, -0.0506, -0.0225, -0.0, -0.0, -0.5387, -0.6949, -0.0021, -0.0042, -0.3632, -0.0024, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0047, -0.0, -0.332, -0.6722, -0.5468, -0.2561, -0.796, -0.8716, -0.0248, -0.0, -0.0541, -0.7114, -0.1199, -0.1269, -0.2391, -0.2049, -0.0111, -0.0, -0.0012, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0298, -0.2131, -0.0, -0.0837, -0.5599, -0.0032, -0.1111, -0.0078, -0.5008, -0.2859, -0.0, -0.2925, -0.0728, -0.6162, -0.3298, -0.0034, -0.3449, -1.0306, -0.0006, -0.3708, -0.2322, -0.0051, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.384, -0.1738, -0.2049, -0.0, -0.3941, -0.079, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.1511, -0.1104, -0.751, -0.2489, -0.4934, -0.0668], "cumulative_logprob": -22.3117, "norm_logp": -0.1923, "input_len": 434, "output_tokens": 116, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.49, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success", "GAVIE-Judge": "glm-4.5-flash"}}
{"id": "702", "pred": "Additionally, teachers can enhance learning by providing engaging materials and asking open-ended questions, modeling vocabulary, encouraging predictions, and observing children's activities.", "raw_output": "Children learn several things through water play:\n\n1. Gross motor skills: Running through sprinklers or filling and pouring water helps develop physical coordination and movement.\n2. Academic learning:\n   - Science: Children learn about floating and sinking and how different objects behave in water.\n   - Math: They learn about measuring, more and less by filling buckets with varying amounts of water.\n3. Creativity: Water play encourages imagination and pretend play.\n\nAdditionally, teachers can enhance learning by providing engaging materials and asking open-ended questions, modeling vocabulary, encouraging predictions, and observing children's activities.", "gt": "Children learn various skills and concepts through water play. They develop gross motor skills by running through sprinklers and pouring water. Water play also provides opportunities for academic learning, such as understanding concepts like floating and sinking, measuring water, and comparing quantities. Additionally, water play encourages creativity and imagination as children pretend and engage in imaginative play with water.", "question": "what do children learn with water play", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "cad", "has_cad": true, "cad_context": "passage", "time_sec": 39.341, "status": "success", "record_process": true, "token_ids": [11539, 3960, 3807, 2513, 1526, 3015, 1486, 1447, 16, 13, 42807, 8872, 7361, 25, 28025, 1526, 50698, 9254, 476, 21274, 323, 50413, 3015, 8609, 2225, 6961, 37693, 323, 7203, 624, 17, 13, 41070, 6832, 510, 256, 481, 9965, 25, 15044, 3960, 911, 19057, 323, 61093, 323, 1246, 2155, 6171, 35692, 304, 3015, 624, 256, 481, 4149, 25, 2379, 3960, 911, 28990, 11, 803, 323, 2686, 553, 21274, 4